WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.449 --> 00:00:08.040
Around 66 million years ago, an asteroid struck
our planet and triggered a mass extinction

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.650
that wiped out all non-avian dinosaurs. But,
what if it hadn’t? What would our world

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.370
look like if the dinosaurs still ruled over
it? This theoretical timeline is the subject

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.770
of the speculative zoology book ‘The New
Dinosaurs’ — written by Dougal Dixon,

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:30.270
who popularized speculative zoology (with
a trilogy which also includes ‘After Man’

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:35.120
and ‘Man After Man,’) that chronicles
evolution in the far future. Dixon’s work

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.480
influenced other series you might know like
the Future is Wild, and C. M. Kosemen's ambitious

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:47.570
‘All Tomorrows.’ But ‘The New Dinosaurs’
is unique in how imagines not the future — but

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:53.050
an alternate form of the present. So, for
this entry into the archive, we’re going

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:58.649
to catalog this other world Dixon conceptualized
— bearing in mind some of the science is

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:03.280
a bit outdated, and it’s of course impossible
to know exactly what such a timeline would

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.119
look like. Think of this book — and by extension,
this video — as just an interesting guess.

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:14.590
So let’s jump in, and imagine the great
asteroid was just another flash of light in

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:22.369
the sky, and over the next several million
years, a dawn rose on a new order of dinosaurs.

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:30.729
Beginning in the continent we know as Africa,
the first ‘new dinosaurs’ we meet are

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:36.959
the strange, forest-dwelling Tree Hoppers
and long-faced waspeaters. The waspeater is

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:42.420
similar to our timeline’s anteater, as both
species spend time in the trees and feature

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:49.030
lengthy snouts for reaching into insect nests.
Dixon classifies these species as Arbosaurs

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:54.240
— and his concept of a tree dwelling dinosaur
actually came true to an extent. After this

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.241
book’s publication, paleontologists discovered
the existence of microraptors: small, tree

00:02:00.241 --> 00:02:06.289
dwelling dinosaurs not far off from what Dixon
imagined. Moving from the forest to the savanna

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:13.040
we’ve got the hilariously named Flarp, bipedal
scavengers descended from the aerial pterosaurs.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.670
Like the emu or ostrich we know, their wings
are now vestigial, so they’re stuck on the

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.620
ground. Even more stuck on the ground is the
Lank — massive, unusual grazers also descended

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:29.269
from pterosaurs. This species is a pretty
clear analogue to the giraffe, and some paleologists

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:35.810
have noted isn’t the most realistic species
Dixon imagined. But the Lank are just so funny

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:41.880
looking, I can’t help but approve. And in
the desert the last African species I want

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:48.290
to touch on awaits. Beneath the sand is the
wyrm — a super alarming-looking organism.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:53.239
Dixon imagines this tube of a creature moves
like a snake, and can burrow under the sands

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:59.200
to catch prey. Frankly, I wonder if our snakes
would be offended by the comparison. Heading

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.659
north to the continent we know as Europe,
is a species even further away from traditional

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:10.030
dinosaurs. This is the Gestalt, a species
similar in size to ground squirrels — but

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:16.760
Dixon imagines they could build complex nests
and even form complex communities. These intelligent

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.120
dinosaurs are descended from Pachycephalosaurus,
thick skulled lizards not really known for

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:27.999
their brains — so it’s an incredible transformation.
Near the Gestalt, we have the diminutive Zwim

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:33.030
— not a dinosaur at all, but a mammal! The
species is close to a modern mouse, and is

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.890
about as big as mammals get in this alternate
reality. (elephant footage). This is because

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:41.249
mammals were only able to diversify into (link
to eocene) the species we know today because

00:03:41.249 --> 00:03:46.940
the dinosaurs declined, which never happened
in this timeline. To the north, we have the

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:53.230
hefty tromble, similar in stature to ostriches.
Like the mammals, there aren’t many species

00:03:53.230 --> 00:04:01.790
of true birds in this alternate reality, which
makes sense considering their competition.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.510
Across the ocean to the region we call North
America, we’ve got the truly insane looking

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.730
Spinosaurus. The surreal dinosaurs are evolved
from the duckbilled dinosaurs of the Cretacecous

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:16.900
period — with elaborate crests used for
group identification. The sprintosaurs are

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:21.420
similar to the pronghorn antelope in they
can run at high speeds to avoid predators,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.410
and have become fully quadrupedal — meaning
they walk on four legs. The sprinosaurs’s

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:32.420
are hunted by the rather deranged looking
northclaw, a therapod with a massive single

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:37.730
claw for hunting prey. But there’s one dinosaur
species on the grassland the northclaw can’t

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:43.530
catch — that being the massive herbivorous
monocorn. These huge grazing animals share

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:48.160
the large, defensive horns of rhinos, but
move across the North American prairies in

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.380
great herds like buffalo. Descended from the
triceratops, they’re maintaining their ancestor’s

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.190
niche as large, defensive herbivores. Heading
to the rainforests of what we term South America,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:04.320
we’ve got the scale-covered pangaloon — a
species with an armored defense similar to

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:11.750
our timeline’s pangolins, who are… just
the best. Nearby, the massive Lumbers continue

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.540
the treetop grazing habits of their sauropod
ancestors, although they now feature a trunk

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:22.450
much like our reality’s tapirs, which both
species use to aid in consuming vegetation.

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:28.010
Tragically, the lumbers are preyed upon by
the fearsome cutlass tooth, a dinosaur whose

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:33.310
unique dental arrangement resembles our timeline’s
sabertooth. Given their frightening nature,

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:38.810
you’d expect the culasstooth to be the T-rex’s
descendant. But nope, the tyrannosaurs rex

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:43.750
evolved into the rather stumpy looking Gourmand
— a species which lacks any front limbs

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:50.000
at all. I mean, hey: in our timeline the tyrannosaurs’
closet living relative is the chicken — so

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.060
it could be worse. Crossing Oceans once again
to what we know as Asia, we’ve got the mountain-dwelling

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:01.821
Taddey: a species covered in insulating-pseudo
features with a rather unfortunate face. The

00:06:01.821 --> 00:06:07.380
Taddey is filing a similar niche to our reality’s
macaque, a species of monkeys which thrive

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:12.570
in similar regions. The Taddey’s coloring
and tree-swinging abilities also make it similar

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:18.110
to the gibbon — although unlike either monkey
species, the Taddey is imagined to not be

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:23.190
particularly smart. Another species that will
haunt my nightmares, the treewryms an elongated

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:28.080
species related to the desert wyrm discussed
in the Africa section. Like the desert variety,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.470
the Treeworm is a snake like predator, and
possesses the ability like many modern snakes

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:38.390
to climb trees and move along branches in
search of prey. Pretty unsettling stuff. In

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:44.120
a distant valley, a less unsettling — but
truly unique — species lurks. This is the

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:50.640
numbskull, a bipedal animal with a swollen-looking
head which seems almost humanoid in its appearance.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.520
The idea of dinosaurs eventually evolving
into a human-like intelligent species seems

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.960
fascinating, and has been the subject of numerous
sci-fi books. As interesting as this seems

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.240
in theory, it’s hard to say how likely it
is a creature with human-like intellect would

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.460
emerge from dinosaurs — and Dixon doesn’t
seem too convinced. This is why the numbskull,

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:16.110
living up to its name, isn’t too bright.
Further south we come to Australia, a region

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:21.150
with plenty of unique animals in our timeline.
And Dixon’s vision of the place is even

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:27.650
crazier. This is the Cribrum, an algae chomping
species similar in build and coloring to the

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:32.090
flamingos. Unlike our reality’s flamingos,
however, the Cribrum can’t actually take

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:36.270
flight. One species that can at least get
some air through hopping is the gwanna, the

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:41.090
last surviving relation to the iganodonts
which hop across the grassy plains. As you

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:45.710
can imagine, these dinosaurs have found a
similar role to that of kangaroos, and are

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:51.800
even a similar size. Much smaller is the crackbeak,
another species with an appearance I find

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:58.700
deeply unsettling. But nature is about function,
not form, and the crackbeak are quite skilled

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:03.740
at cracking open nuts — although the species
also eats insects, much like the modern Australian

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:09.540
kookaburra. And our final Australian dinosaur
of note is the Kloon, a strangely endearing

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:14.940
bipedal herbivore with a thick wooly coat.
Although not filling the same niche, the animal

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:22.770
reminds me of the capybara, which has a similar
face and peaceful nature. But we’re not

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:27.760
done with our list quite yet. We still need
to explore what sorts of species are inhabiting

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:33.469
this world’s oceans. Beginning near the
poles, we have the aquatic plunger — a streamlined

00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:38.800
black and white organism that’s a pretty
clear analogue to the penguin. Like most penguins,

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.820
they’re described as adept swimmers with
wings modified for hydrodynamic movement.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:49.460
A pretty creative idea for a species, if a
familiar one. Far less familiar, however,

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:56.180
is the Kraken, an aptly-named species with
a shell that resembles a tiny floating island.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.570
The kraken had twelve tentless with deadly
stingers, and will eat anything that becomes

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:09.230
entangled in its traps. If anything, the Kraken
resembles our timeline’s nautilus, although

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:15.730
they aren’t nearly as fantastical. And the
last species we’re touching on is by far

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:22.300
the largest: this is the Whulk, a huge leviathan
which is a descendant of the ichthyosaur that

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:28.840
evolved to eat plankton. The massive species
is a direct analogue to our world’s whales,

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:37.230
sharing similar feeding habits and gargantuan
proportions. And that’s the end of Dougal

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:41.340
Dixon’s ‘The New Dinosaurs’ and our
window into an alternate timeline where dinosaurs

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:52.680
still ruled the earth. Thanks for watching.
If you liked this entry, please lend your

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:59.390
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:19.940
See you in the next video.

